        SGREP is a modification of the public domainversion of the Unix utility GREP, PC Sig #149 or CUG 152.The following additions were made:        1) string substitution capability,        2) multiple pattern search,        3) distinction between upper and lower case,        4) scanning options to extend capability and speed-up processing.USAGE-----------------------------------------------------------------Sgrep searches a file for a given pattern and substitutes a pattern.If no substitution is desired, specify the -m switch.Sgrep operates as a filter and may be used with redirectors or pipes.If the output is not redirected or piped, it will be sent to the screen. Ifno redirection or piping is done for the input, sgrep will sit there waitingfor input from the keyboard. Terminate with the eof character (ctrl-Break forOS9) when entering information from the keyboard.SYNTAX----------------------------------------------------------------  sgrep [-cmnvy] pattern-file <inputpath >outputpathWhere:    c  =  Only a count of matching lines is printed    m  =  Match patterns only, no substitutions.    n  =  Each line is preceeded by its line number    v  =  Only print non-matching lines    y  =  Upper and lower case match.(The c,n and v switches are only valid when the m switch is used.)The switches listed above are optional.  If no switches are specified, sgrepwill proceed to process the data stream, with upper/lower casedifferentiation and will substitute the replacement pattern for any targetpatterns found.PATTER FILE FORMAT----------------------------------------------------Each match pattern is on a separate line followed by its substitutepattern, if any, also on a separate line.|--------------------------------------------------------------||                                                              ||eq: (match-only pattern file)                                 ||                                                              ||fish   (target pattern)                                       ||frog   (target pattern)                                       ||                                                              ||  (note the difference between the match-only pattern file    ||   and the substitution pattern file described below.)        ||                                                              ||--------------------------------------------------------------|MATCH PATTERNS--------------------------------------------------------A Regular Expression (match pattern) is defined according to the followingrules:Upper and lowercase are regarded as different unless -y option is used.Characters are matched directly except for the following control characters.'\'    The backslash quotes any character.  "\$" matches a dollar-sign.       (The backslash is used to set a direct match for the control       characters.)'$'    Matches beginning or end of line.'.'    A period matches any character except "new-line".':a'   A colon matches a class of characters described by the following':d'     character.  ":a" matches any alphabetic, ":d" matches digits,':n'     ":n" matches alphanumerics, ": " matches spaces, tabs, and': '     other control characters except new-line.'*'    An expression followed by an asterisk matches zero or more       occurances of that expression: "fo*" matches "f", "fo"       "foo", etc.'+'    An expression followed by a plus sign matches one or more       occurances of that expression: "fo+" matches "fo", etc.'-'    An expression followed by a minus sign optionally matches       the expression.'[]'   A string enclosed in square brackets matches any character in       that string, but no others.  If the first character in the       string is a circumflex, the expression matches any character       except "new-line" and the characters in the string.  For       example, "[xyz]" matches "xx" and "zyx", while "[^xyz]"       matches "abc" but not "axb".  A range of characters may be       specified by two characters separated by "-".  Note that,       [a-z] matches alphabetics, while [z-a] never matches.The concatenation of Regular Expressions is a Regular Expression.SCANNING OPTIONS------------------------------------------------------The default scanning option is match all occurences of the pattern.'@'    at the beginning of the pattern, is equivalent to       "$: *", meaning match first non-whitespace pattern.'@e'   at the end of a pattern means that if a match is       found (and a substitution made), end all pattern search       on current line.'@r'   at the end of a pattern means that after a match       (and substitution), rescan line until no match occurs.SUBSTITUTION PATTERNS------------------------------------------------Substitution patterns are effective when the -m switch is NOT specified. Asubstitution pattern file must have an EVEN number of entries, with each oddentry being the pattern to search for, and each following even entry beingthe pattern to substitute. Each search pattern and each substitution patternmust be on its own individual line.|---------------------------------------------------------------------||                                                                     ||eg:    (file for pattern substition)                                 ||                                                                     ||fish            (target pattern)                                     ||aquatic animal  (substitution pattern)                               ||                                                                     ||(This will replace all occurances of "fish" with "aquatic animal".)  ||(Compare this file example to the example for a match-only pattern   || file as described above.)                                           ||                                                                     ||---------------------------------------------------------------------|The only control character for substitution patterns is "?".Any other character represents itself. Only the characters ? and \ (the quote character- as above)  need to be quoted.'?n'   where n is a non-zero digit, indicates the position where       the nth wildcard string is to be placed.A wildcard string is any string which is not completely fixed,both as to the characters and number of characters in the string.    1) When '$' is the first character in the pattern it represents the    BEGINNING of a line.  If it in any other position it represents the END    of a line.    2) The scan options described above have been added. The @ and @eoptions speed-up processing. The @r option allows certain repetitveoperations. For example, the standard array index designation  A[I,J,K] is changed to the C form A[I][J][K] by the pattern pair        \[:n+,@r        [?1][This will handle arrays of any number of dimensions. The @r scan optionis also used to insert the & before each item in a list of input variables.CAUTIONS--------------------------------------------------------------1) If you are running a match only operation (-m switch specified), make surethat the pattern file is set up as a match-only pattern file.If you are running a substitution operation (-m switch NOT specified), makesure that the pattern file is set up as a substitution pattern file.(See the differences in these file types as described above.)2) If spaces are inadvertently inserted at the END of a pattern in thepattern file, those spaces will NOT be ignored by sgrep.  Spaces placed atthe end of a target pattern will necessitate any occurances of the targetpattern in the input to HAVE THOSE SPACES.  Spaces placed at the end of asubstitution pattern will BE INSERTED into the output.   Any spaces at the BEGINNING of a pattern will be treated in a like manner,but the ones at the end will be the ones that aren't obvious.         